## Burgundy vs Maroon vs Wine vs Oxblood: Which Deep Red Are You Actually After?

Nobody tells you that "burgundy" covers at least four different shades on a nail wall, and that picking the wrong one is why you sometimes end up with something that reads too brown, too purple, or not quite the wine red you had in your head.

The names all live in the same deep red family, but they are not the same colour. Burgundy, the true reference, takes its name from the wine region of France. It is a deep red with a blue-purple undertone: richer than a flat red, more vibrant than a maroon, with a cool depth that reads as sophisticated rather than earthy. This is the classic "glass of wine on a dark evening" shade. For a reference point on the red end of the spectrum, Red Nails: Bold, Classic & Always in Style shows where burgundy ends and true red begins.

Maroon pulls in a different direction. It blends red with brown, which gives it a warmer, earthier character. Maroon does not have the cool sophistication of burgundy. It reads more grounded, more rustic, more autumn-leaf than Bordeaux-glass. On nails, maroon can look incredible but it will not deliver the same wine-dark depth that most people are searching for when they ask for burgundy.

Oxblood goes darker and earthier still. It was originally the fashion term for a deep reddish-brown leather, and on nails it sits noticeably deeper than burgundy. It is less purple, more close-to-black-red. It is the most dramatic member of the family, with a heaviness that makes it genuinely vampy rather than simply dark.

Bordeaux and wine are largely interchangeable with each other and with the true burgundy reference. Wine tends to be used for slightly warmer versions; bordeaux implies the most refined, slightly plum-adjacent interpretation. They are close enough that the finish will matter more than the name.

So when you are at the salon or staring at a nail wall: if you want the classic rich wine-red, ask for burgundy or bordeaux. If you want something earthier and warmer, ask for maroon. If you want something close to almost-black-red, oxblood is your shade.

