## The Burgundy Shade Guide: Matching Your Undertone to the Right Wine Red

If your <strong>burgundy nails</strong> have ever read muddy, flat, or somehow wrong against your skin. The shade was almost certainly the problem, not you.

Undertones determine which burgundy works and which one fights your complexion. The quickest way to identify yours: look at the veins on your inner wrist in natural light. Blue-purple veins indicate cool undertones. Green veins indicate warm undertones. A mix of both means neutral undertones, which gives you the widest range to work with.

**Cool undertones** (the blue-purple vein camp) are served best by a burgundy that leans into its purple-blue base. True wine reds, cranberry-adjacent shades, and any burgundy described as "bordeaux" or "plum-adjacent" will look genuinely luminous here. The cool base in the shade brings out the pink and blue notes in fair to medium cool skin rather than fighting them. <a href='https://voguescandinavia.com/articles/best-burgundy-nail-polishes-for-fall' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Vogue Scandinavia's undertone-matching guide</a> identifies this as the skin tone that gets the most from a true wine burgundy. The shade and the complexion sharpen each other.

**Warm undertones** (the green vein camp) need to push away from the cool-purple end of the burgundy family. A burgundy with a red-mahogany base, a shade described as "deep wine with warm undertones" or "dark cherry," will flatter here. Strictly cool, blue-based burgundies can look slightly off against warm-toned skin, not wrong exactly, but lacking the richness the shade is capable of. Push toward the warmer, redder side of the family.

**Olive undertones** sit between warm and cool, and deep burgundy is one of their best shades. The depth in the colour complements olive skin's earthy character. Both warm and cool burgundies can work; if in doubt, a true mid-burgundy (neither pulling purple nor pulling brown) is the safe call.

**Deep and dark skin tones** are where <strong>burgundy nails</strong> achieve something remarkable. The warm red-wine depth of a true dark burgundy creates a jewel-like relationship with deeper complexions. The colour appears more saturated and luminous than it does on any other skin tone. For deep skin, the key direction is toward a warm red-based burgundy rather than a cool purple-leaning one. A shade that reads "dark red" rather than "dark purple" will give the richest result.

