## Matte vs Glossy vs Chrome: How Your Finish Changes Everything

Finish is the decision. Everything else comes second: shade, shape, length.

The same burgundy gel polish applied in three different finishes produces three genuinely different manicures. Understanding this is what separates a good burgundy from a great one.

**Glossy** is the classic reference and the most forgiving. A high-shine topcoat over a deep wine red gives you the finish that most people picture when they say <strong>burgundy nails</strong>: rich, liquid-looking, the colour at its most saturated. Glossy burgundy reads as polished in the original sense of the word. It catches light in a way that keeps dark shades from feeling flat, which is important: without shine, a deep red can read heavy. Two thin coats of a well-pigmented gel polish and a quality high-shine topcoat refreshed every three days is all this look requires.

**Matte** is the finish that has built the most momentum in 2026. A matte topcoat transforms burgundy from "polished" to "editorial". The colour becomes velvet-like, textured in feel if not in fact, and the depth of the shade becomes the entire story. Matte burgundy reads as intentionally sophisticated in a way that glossy cannot always achieve. The one caveat: matte finishes show oil from the skin faster than glossy, so cuticle oil goes on the skin only, not the nail.

**Chrome** is the most technically demanding but arguably the most extraordinary result. A burgundy chrome powder applied over a sticky gel base catches light differently with every movement. You get a mirror-like metallic dimension that shifts between deep red and burnished copper depending on the angle. Chrome burgundy is the 2026 nail trend that makes people ask what you are wearing. The application is specific: gel polish must be cured under UV, the tacky layer is what the chrome powder bonds to, and a no-wipe topcoat seals the finish. Reapply the topcoat every few days to maintain the mirror effect.

The <em>cat eye</em> finish deserves a mention in this section. It is technically a chrome variant, using a magnetic powder applied while the gel is still uncured. Dark shades are where the cat eye effect is most dramatic; the shift of light across a deep burgundy base is hypnotic. The magnet is held above the wet gel for 10–15 seconds to pull the pigment into its signature stripe.

