## Short Burgundy Nails: Why Length Has Nothing to Do with It

Dark colours do not need length. That is the conventional wisdom, and conventional wisdom is wrong.

What dark colours need is density: rich, opaque pigment applied evenly over a clean, well-shaped nail. On a short squoval or short almond, <strong>burgundy nails</strong> look precise and deliberate in a way that longer nails sometimes cannot achieve. A long nail with dark polish can read dramatic; a short nail with dark polish reads intentional. Neither is better. They are different aesthetics.

The practical argument for short burgundy is actually stronger than for long: the colour does not show tip wear against itself the way light shades show it against the nail's free edge. A deep wine red on a short, well-maintained tip holds its visual impact longer.

Nail shape matters more on short nails than the length itself. A short squoval, filed square with softly rounded edges, gives a clean, architectural finish that suits professional settings and makes <strong>burgundy gel nails</strong> look salon-done even at home. A short almond elongates the finger more than a square and gives the wine red a slightly more dramatic feel. Rounded shapes on short nails work well with simpler finishes; they can look slightly unresolved with heavy nail art.

The best short nail art for burgundy: negative space swirls (a single curved line of bare nail through the colour), a micro French tip in gold or dusty rose, or a single small accent detail on one ring finger nail. The restraint suits the scale.

