## How to Make Burgundy Nails Last (and Look Expensive While They Do)

The manicure that lifts at the edges by day five is not a product problem. It is a prep problem.

Every professional nail technician will say the same thing: nail prep accounts for the majority of how long a gel manicure lasts. For <strong>burgundy gel nails</strong> specifically: where any lifting shows as a pale crescent against a deep background, the prep stage is not optional. Before any base coat touches the nail, the surface needs to be dehydrated: an alcohol wipe, a light buff to remove shine from the nail plate, and cuticle pushed back cleanly so the gel bonds directly to the nail rather than to any lifted skin at the base.

Application technique follows from there. Thin coats cure more completely than thick ones, and under-cured gel is the second most common cause of early chipping. Two thin coats of a well-pigmented burgundy with a full cure between each coat, plus 10 seconds of additional curing time as a habit, which produces a gel manicure that should last 2–3 weeks without lifting.

<a href='https://opi.com' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>OPI Malaga Wine</a> remains the reference-point <strong>burgundy nail polish</strong> for a reason: the pigment is dense enough that two coats deliver full opacity, the formula is balanced for longevity, and the shade is the most universally flattering true wine red in a standard nail collection. It is not the only option, but it is the benchmark.

The aftercare habits that extend wear: cuticle oil daily (applied to the skin around the nail, not the nail itself), gloves for prolonged water exposure or cleaning products, and a fresh layer of topcoat every 3–4 days. The topcoat refresh is the single most underused manicure tip. It adds 4–5 additional days of wear and maintains the gloss or matte finish at its best quality.

Dark polishes show tip wear earlier than nudes because the contrast at the free edge is visible. A clean topcoat sealed over the tip edge at application (called "capping the free edge") reduces this significantly.

